# Which paths need the repository owner's approval before merging to main.
#
# This is deliberately NOT `*`, and deliberately narrower than "anything
# executable". `main-review` sets `required_approving_review_count: 0`, so a
# pull request touching only paths absent from this file merges on its checks
# alone; one touching any path here still stops for the owner.
#
# The line drawn is **authority, not risk**. Product and control-plane code is
# risky, but it is gated by things that scale: the hosted source gates, and
# AGENTS.md rule 2's adversarial review, which is now a required status check
# reading an exact-head verdict recorded through the maintainer App. Owner
# attention does not scale, so it is spent on the changes those gates cannot
# judge -- the ones that change what agents are *allowed* to do, including the
# gates themselves.
#
# Measured across the eleven pull requests merged on 2026-08-24 (UTC): under
# the blanket approval count of 1 then in force, all eleven waited on the
# owner. Under this file, four of the eleven would -- #321, #323, #345, #367 --
# and each of those four changed CI, the agent rules, or an authority script
# named below.
#
# Two properties make this safe, and both must hold. If either stops being
# true, widen this file again:
#
#  1. `review` is in the `required` aggregate, so no change reaches `main`
#     without a verdict bound to its exact head. Until that is so, a narrow
#     CODEOWNERS leaves nothing standing where review should stand.
#  2. The maintainer App cannot publish under `.github/workflows/`, so an
#     agent-authored change cannot rewrite the gate that judges it. Note the
#     App's refusal is by named path -- `.github/actions/**` IS publishable --
#     which is why the whole `.github` tree is owned here rather than just
#     `workflows/`.
#
# What this accepts, stated plainly: the reviewing and authoring agents reach
# the App through one credential, so a recorded verdict is evidence that a
# review happened, not proof that it was earned. Independence is a property of
# the factory running a fresh agent that did not write the change.

# CI, deployment, repository settings, and this file. A change here can rewrite
# the gate that reviews it, so it is the one path where owner approval is not
# an opinion about quality but a control on authority.
/.github/               @baziyer

# The rules agents operate under, and the boundary they are held to. An agent
# that could amend these could amend its own limits.
/AGENTS.md              @baziyer
/CLAUDE.md              @baziyer
/SECURITY.md            @baziyer
/ARCHITECTURE.md        @baziyer

# The authority boundary in executable form, owned by name. `scripts/` at
# large is deliberately unowned -- it merges on the review verdict like the
# rest of the tree -- but these four publish the ruleset, verify the recorded
# verdict, or causally test those two, so a change to them changes what the
# gate IS. ci.yml already names the hazard a weakening here would realize:
# rule 2 silently unenforced with no red check anywhere. The publisher applies
# itself the next time the operator runs it, and the queue runs the default
# branch's copy of the verifier -- hoisting decides which copy runs, not who
# may change it.
/scripts/github-repo-settings.sh           @baziyer
/scripts/test-repository-settings.sh       @baziyer
/scripts/verify-adversarial-review.sh      @baziyer
/scripts/test-verify-adversarial-review.sh @baziyer
